{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAs behooves any intelligent schoolboy, Kevin Sun is studying psycowlogy, cowculus, and cryptcowgraphy at the Bovinia State University (BGU) under Farmer Ivan. During his Mathematics of Olympiads (MoO) class, Kevin was confronted with a weird functional equation and needs your help. For two fixed integers k and p, where p is an odd prime number, the functional equation states that  for some function . (This equation should hold for any integer x in the range 0 to p\u2009-\u20091, inclusive.)It turns out that f can actually be many different functions. Instead of finding a solution, Kevin wants you to count the number of distinct functions f that satisfy this equation. Since the answer may be very large, you should print your result modulo 109\u2009+\u20097.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\np, k = [int(i) for i in raw_input().split()]\nord_k = 1\nwhile pow(k, ord_k, p) != 1:\n    ord_k += 1\ncomps = (p - 1) / ord_k\nMOD = 1000000007\nif k == 1:\n    result = pow(p, p, MOD)\nelse:\n    result = pow(p, comps, MOD)\nprint result\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\np, k = [int(i) for i in raw_input().split()]\nMOD = 1000000007\n\n# Handle special case when p = 2 (though problem says p is odd prime, so this may be unnecessary)\nif p == 2:\n    if k % 2 == 0:\n        # k \u2261 0 (mod 2), then equation becomes f(x) = f(0) for all x\n        # So f(0) can be any value, f(1) must equal f(0), so p choices\n        result = p % MOD\n    else:\n        # k \u2261 1 (mod 2), then equation becomes f(x) = f(x) which is always true\n        # So any function works: p^p choices\n        result = pow(p, p, MOD)\nelse:\n    # Find the multiplicative order of k modulo p\n    # The order is the smallest positive integer d such that k^d \u2261 1 (mod p)\n    ord_k = 1\n    current = k % p\n    while current != 1:\n        ord_k += 1\n        current = (current * k) % p\n        if ord_k > p:  # Safety check\n            break\n    \n    # The functional equation is f(x) = f(k*x mod p)\n    # This creates equivalence classes: x ~ k*x ~ k^2*x ~ ... \n    # For x = 0: 0 ~ 0 ~ 0 ... so {0} is a singleton class\n    # For x \u2260 0: the orbit size is the order of k in the multiplicative group\n    \n    # Number of equivalence classes:\n    # - One class for {0}\n    # - (p-1)/ord_k classes for the nonzero elements\n    \n    num_classes = 1 + (p - 1) // ord_k\n    \n    # Each equivalence class can be assigned any value from 0 to p-1 independently\n    result = pow(p, num_classes, MOD)\n\nprint result\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-9.536738616588991e-07, -2.098061486321967e-05, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -0.17891596257686615, -1.0967194612021558e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -2.3841574147809297e-05, -3.576278118089249e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -2.086140739265